+
+Table~\ref{tab:exp5} summarises medians across policy sizes and
+Fig.~\ref{fig:exp5} plots the same data with $95\%$ bootstrap CIs.
+At every operating point U-HAP completes the update cycle faster than
+OPA, and the gap widens with policy count: from $1.63\times$ at $n{=}10$
+to $2.73\times$ at $n{=}2000$. On the engine-only post-parse path---the
+apples-to-apples comparison---the advantage reaches $19.5\times$ at
+$n{=}2000$ ($31$\,ms vs.\ $611$\,ms), reflecting the asymptotic benefit
+of compile-time hash consing and indexed artifacts over OPA's
+per-update Rego compilation. We additionally measured an OPA
+\emph{bundle activation} mode (\texttt{opa build -b} followed by REST
+upload), which yields results within $\pm 4\%$ of the REST PUT mode at
+every $n$, confirming the gap is not a deployment-mode artefact. The
+breakdown in Fig.~\ref{fig:exp5_breakdown} shows that U-HAP's update
+cost is dominated by the compile phase that produces the hash-consed
+DAG and the per-($n$,$r$,$a$) indexed artifacts; the compiled-state
+memory footprint grows from $0.1$\,MiB at $n{=}10$ to $2.7$\,MiB at
+$n{=}2000$, well within the operating budget of an in-cluster admission
+webhook.
+
+\noindent\textbf{Single-replica scope.} The numbers above measure the
+per-replica swap latency. In a multi-replica deployment, a global
+update also incurs a propagation step that depends on the chosen
+distribution mechanism (Kubernetes \texttt{ConfigMap}\,+\,reload, Git
+pull, or a control-plane push). Because that propagation cost is
+identical for U-HAP and OPA when both consume the same delivery
+substrate, the per-replica numbers reported here are the right
+primitive for comparing the two engines, and the observed
+$2.73\times$ swap-time advantage carries through to the
+post-propagation consistency window.
